Calc: an .xlsx import or export gives the memory it freed back to the system (glibc malloc_trim).

Importing a workbook allocates and frees many short-lived objects between the long-lived cells of the document,
and glibc keeps that free space resident. After opening a 50 MB workbook of formulas about 250 MB of the process
was such free space (anonymous memory 1.70 GB after the open, 1.45 GB after malloc_trim(0)); a 200 MB workbook
of formulas held 6.6 GB after the open, which left too little of 8 GB for saving it. malloc_trim(0) after the
import and after the export returns the free pages; nothing moves and the allocator stays the same.
Only with glibc (nothing elsewhere). The environment variable HST_SC_XLSX_TRIM_OFF disables it.
New logic in sc/source/filter/inc/hstreleasefreedmemory.hxx; hooks: 2 calls in ExcelFilter::filter.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new file is LicenseRef-HST-Proprietary.
Test: CppunitTest_sc_subsequent_export_test6 testXlsxImportAndExportReleaseFreedMemory (the switch, and an .xlsx
round trip of formulas through both hooks).
---
